WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.455
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.784 --> 00:00:13.200
PRESENTER: I am
pleased to welcome

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.700
to the platform the
Honorable Alfred E. Vellucci,

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.470
mayor of the city of Cambridge,
and Dr. A. Bartlett Giamatti,

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:26.970
president of the National League
of Professional Baseball Clubs,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:30.300
and former president
of Yale University.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.385
Dr. Giamatti will
now give the address.

00:00:35.628 --> 00:00:38.118
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.660
GIAMATTI: Good morning.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.260
I know that this
occasion is a solemn one.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:55.680
It is meant to celebrate the
graduates, meant to affirm us

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.070
and our commitment to
seeking the truth, meant

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.420
to figure forth the
institution's role as conveyor

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.430
of that common life
we call civilization.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.990
I've thought much
on our institutions

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.240
for learning, our universities
in the last two years,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.850
and today I want to
share with you, if I

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:18.000
may, some of those ruminations.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.230
Specifically, I wish
to ruminate on what

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:25.570
it means to be a university
president, which I once was.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.460
What does this have to
do with you graduates?

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.740
Well, aside from
enriching this moment

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.530
and of course, your
lives in general,

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.360
my ruminations will
eventually prove

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:36.900
to be splendidly relevant.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.800
Being president of a
university is no way

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.890
for an adult to
make a living, which

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:49.780
is why so few adults
actually attempt to do it.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:52.820
It is to hold a mid-19th
century ecclesiastical

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.630
position on top of a
late-20th century corporation.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:00.420
But there are those
lucid moments,

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.990
those Joycean epiphanies
that occur and lay bare

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.860
the numinous beyond and give
us the essence of it all.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.030
I had those moments.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.330
They were all moments of
profound and brilliant failure,

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:18.610
but string those moments
of defeat into a strand

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:21.130
and you have the pearls of
an administrative career.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.350
Six months between being
named president of Yale

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:31.810
in December of '77 and
taking office in July of '78,

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.170
I had ample opportunity
to receive advice.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.751
I listened to many people.

00:02:36.751 --> 00:02:38.500
I learned for instance,
for the first time

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.690
about the corporate world.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.030
I learned that because
the corporate world is

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.010
only interested in
quarterly results,

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:47.080
it talks a great deal
about long-range planning.

00:02:49.732 --> 00:02:53.240
It was very clear to me that
Yale needed some of that too.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.770
We needed a policy.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.130
I, of course had no policies.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:58.960
I had a mortgage,
and I had one suit,

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.610
but I had absolutely no policy.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:03.600
So I cast about.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.300
I solicited data and forecasts
and projections and models.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.150
I did something called
a comparative study.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:10.920
I did longitudinal studies.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.330
I made a flowchart, and I fired
four management consultants.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:16.590
I went in search of policy.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.630
[CHEERING]

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.880
You got it.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.210
I was trying to find what
it was Yale needed most,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.730
wanted most, and
would most contribute

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.070
to enhancing our
quality, and making me

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.730
what I knew now I was to
be, which was a manager.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:36.436
Well, one night in April of '78
I was in my garage, actually.

00:03:36.436 --> 00:03:38.810
I was trying to memorize the
trustees names, particularly

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:40.260
the ones I'd met.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:48.070
And I was crouched between the
lawnmower and some snow tires,

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:48.950
and I wrote a memo.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.930
It was the first time
I'd ever written.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.820
On July 1, 1978, which
my first day in office,

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.300
I issued the following memo
to an absent and indifferent

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:57.930
university.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.240
And it read as follows--

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.470
"To the members of the
university community.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.810
In order to repair
what Milton called

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:09.410
the ruin of our
grandparents, I wish

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:13.460
to announce that henceforth, as
a matter of university policy,

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.649
evil is abolished and
paradise is restored."

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:22.160
Well, the reaction
was fascinating.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:23.650
Four young members
of the faculty

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:26.200
in comparative literature
wrote an open letter

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.690
to the New York Review of Books
proving the Milton was not

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:31.010
talking about evil in
Paradise Lost, he was in fact,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:34.240
talking about irony and the
patriarchal abuse of power.

00:04:37.885 --> 00:04:39.510
There was a junior
in Yale College, who

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:42.150
was doing that summer,
a leveraged buyout

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.990
of a Tastee-Freez
in East Hampton.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.490
She wrote me a very
gracious letter,

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.031
and said she understood that
one had to have a business plan,

00:04:50.031 --> 00:04:51.810
but she hoped I
didn't change things

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.630
too much before she graduated.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:58.140
Of course, there was the
alumnus in New York on Yale club

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.820
stationery who
wondered why the heck

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.800
we always had to get
so far out in front.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.404
In September, an undergraduate
extracurricular activity

00:05:07.404 --> 00:05:08.820
in New Haven called
the Yale Daily

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:12.594
News wrote the first
editorial about my memo.

00:05:12.594 --> 00:05:14.010
It's opening
sentences were these.

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:15.570
I remember them.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:19.560
"Giamatti's administration
is off to a miserable start.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.567
Rather than giving us
control over our lives,

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:23.400
or at least addressing
concerns of students,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.858
such as the crying need for a
student center so we can make

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.110
friends, or any of the other
myriad of injustices that

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:31.620
riddle the fabric of the
quality of life here,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.680
new administration is
insensitive and repressive

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.958
and the future bodes awful."

00:05:37.958 --> 00:05:42.460
And that was one of the best
written of the news editorials.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:44.850
But to be fair, of course,
it was also the first.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.570
Well, since the
students were back,

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.720
and this Daily
News is publishing,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.990
the major media outlets
now had a source for news,

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:55.290
because student
stringers went to work.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:59.590
In a small article byline
special to the New York Times,

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.511
the country's newspaper of
record misspelled my name

00:06:03.511 --> 00:06:06.010
and said, a Harvard professor
had found a letter from Milton

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:09.340
to his parents in
[INAUDIBLE] library.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:11.440
The Washington
Post ran a picture

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.420
of the memo in
the style section,

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:18.410
and wrote a sidebar in a box
quoting an FDA lawyer, who

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:21.600
asserted that evil had been
abolished three years earlier.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.330
The regulations
that, after all have

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.529
been printed in the
Federal Register,

00:06:25.529 --> 00:06:28.070
and nobody he knew in Washington
thought evil was bad for you

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:30.146
in any case.

00:06:30.146 --> 00:06:33.920
The Wall Street Journal
wrote a very pithy editorial,

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:38.390
pointing out that fat, liberal,
effete, Marxist-oriented

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:42.550
eastern universities,
and Stanford too,

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:45.240
were all in a plot to
undermine the republic

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.910
and free enterprise.

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:48.890
Quote, "What we need,
they said in the journal,

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:51.710
is not more talk about evil,
but some decent courses

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.060
in risk arbitrage.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.590
George Will wrote
another column citing

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:02.210
Montesquieu, Thomas Aquinas,
Locke, and Ernie Banks.

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:06.810
William Buckley said Milton,
quote "is all very well,

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:11.660
but it is typical of
President Giamatti and his ilk

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:14.330
to cite a secular
authority on evil

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.700
as if, of course,
those who have passed

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:20.450
any time down in the
agora, or out on the Rialto

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:23.840
needed an authority to know
the palpability of evil

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:26.970
in all its camaraderie
and liberal camouflages."

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:29.330
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.640
Well, it goes on.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.830
I won't go on.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:36.889
As you know, a
university president

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:39.180
has a responsibility not only
for the internal workings

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.760
of an institution, but also
for external representation

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.040
and relations as well.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.340
And of all the moments I
remember speaking to alumni

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.090
and visiting foundations
and corporations and mayors

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:52.184
and governors and et
cetera, et cetera,

00:07:52.184 --> 00:07:54.850
the moment I remember best-- and
I just reminded President Gray,

00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:57.730
I think we met in this office
that morning in Washington--

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:00.100
was the morning I saw
Congressman Phlange

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:02.605
from the third district of a
state that we will call grace.

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.660
Congressman's office-- let
me set the scene briefly--

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:11.235
is a series of dark
paneled warrens each

00:08:11.235 --> 00:08:12.110
leading to the other.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.880
And as one enters,
one sees on the wall

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:17.060
a framed poster of
the last major arts

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:22.824
festival held in the district,
which was August 17, 1937.

00:08:22.824 --> 00:08:24.740
There are some chairs
at the table with copies

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.370
of Collier's, and a telephone
that doesn't call anything.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.500
There is, of course no ashtray.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.690
The first receptionist is
reading her high school

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:34.336
yearbook and drinking
a diet Sprite,

00:08:34.336 --> 00:08:36.169
so I approach the other
one who is less busy

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:45.697
and I say, "Mr. Giamatti to
see the congressman, please."

00:08:45.697 --> 00:08:48.030
She looks up and says, well,
he's either in the district

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:48.696
or on the floor.

00:08:48.696 --> 00:08:51.645
They're not sure, while I
sit in the corner by a phone.

00:08:51.645 --> 00:08:54.120
And suddenly, the inner door
opens and a middle-aged person

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:56.190
with eyeglasses, hung on a
green cord around her neck,

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:57.720
and carrying up an
appointment book,

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.761
a clipboard, a stack of
letters, a cup of coffee,

00:08:59.761 --> 00:09:02.270
and a Snoopy lunchbox,
comes up to me and says,

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:03.270
he'll see you right now.

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:03.978
Please follow me.

00:09:03.978 --> 00:09:06.480
And she takes me out the door,
down the hall to the right,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.550
through the first
door we come to,

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:11.030
passed the word processor
and an empty desk,

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.750
down a short corridor filled
with overflowing waste boxes.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:16.724
She goes sharp right, past
the young man methodically

00:09:16.724 --> 00:09:18.390
shredding what looks
like mail, and then

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:21.480
into the congressman's office.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.260
Well, the congressman is sitting
behind a huge desk, surrounded

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.230
by plaques and awards,
and trophies and pictures

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:31.680
and laminated scrolls, and
six autographed footballs.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.500
There are easy chairs, a
chocolate colored wastebasket,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:36.270
an American flag,
and a mother of pearl

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:37.920
paperweight the
size of a basketball

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.050
with Republic of China
written across the bottom.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:43.834
Doctor, he says, how are you?

00:09:43.834 --> 00:09:44.500
It's a pleasure.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:45.150
Please sit down.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.230
Can we get you some coffee?

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:47.479
What brings you to Washington?

00:09:47.479 --> 00:09:50.027
He of course, has
not yet looked up.

00:09:50.027 --> 00:09:51.360
I'd like to get a picture of us.

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:52.260
I'll find a photographer.

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:53.610
And suddenly, he's
gone, out the door,

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:55.235
and then he's back
with a photographer.

00:09:55.235 --> 00:09:58.290
And with a tall,
slim woman, about 30,

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:01.890
in slacks, blue work
shirt, denim vest, boots,

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:04.590
her hair pulled back in a bun.

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:07.380
Doctor, he says, this is
Mrs. incomparable Worth,

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:09.450
my legislative
assistant for education.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.600
She'll sit in.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:11.421
A flash goes off.

00:10:11.421 --> 00:10:12.420
The photographer leaves.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:15.220
And Ms Worth now speaks.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:18.940
She says, we thank the NIH
cuts should go through.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:20.970
We're not impressed with
your fatuous argument

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:24.230
that we can't change the rules
halfway through the game.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:26.490
We think student aid only
benefits the rich and poor,

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:28.448
and rather than stopping
abuse, we would rather

00:10:28.448 --> 00:10:30.630
do away with everything.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.940
We don't believe in a federal
science facilities fund,

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:35.724
or in fact, in the
non-profit postal subsidy.

00:10:35.724 --> 00:10:37.890
And given what they teach
in comparative literature,

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:39.973
we think it would be the
height of fraud and abuse

00:10:39.973 --> 00:10:40.950
to fund the humanities.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:45.000
We intend to uncap retirement,
cap technology transfer,

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.156
cut the NSF, get rid of
the Library of Congress,

00:10:47.156 --> 00:10:49.530
and slash the Health Manpower
Act, because we want to get

00:10:49.530 --> 00:10:50.640
this country moving again.

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.090
Well, congressman beams, and he
says, doctor, let me tell you.

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.840
It's an honor having you here.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.380
We've got a college
in the district.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:00.850
They do a wonderful job.

00:11:00.850 --> 00:11:03.001
The education is marvelous.

00:11:03.001 --> 00:11:04.500
Look what it made
the country today.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.740
We've got a huge deficit on
balance trade, a weak dollar,

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:12.194
corruption in church and state,
although separated, of course.

00:11:12.194 --> 00:11:13.110
It's great to see you.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:14.670
Anything I can do,
just let me know.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:15.750
Well, I go back out.

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:17.520
I go past the young
man shredding,

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.410
past the waste baskets, past
the silent word processor.

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:22.170
I'm finally in the hall.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.580
I had not said a word.

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:26.050
But I had done
what I came to do,

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:30.160
which is I had my picture
taken, I'd seen a staffer,

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:32.950
I'd met a congressman, and I'd
heard all the issues touched

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.630
definitively.

00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:37.330
I felt that our
system is working,

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:39.710
and the visit remains
in the mind as a pearl.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:44.770
There's only one other moment
that I want to share with you.

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:48.020
It's a brief, but
glistening session.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:49.940
Not long before I
left in '86, with

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:54.080
a university-wide,
community-based, self-selected

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:58.690
group called the Standing
Committee on Special Interests.

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:02.790
This committee is the
special interest group

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:06.580
that convenes to pursue a
special interests if there's

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:10.390
no preexisting special interest
group empowered to pursue

00:12:10.390 --> 00:12:13.210
that special interest.

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:16.060
It monitors public utterances
to see who might be offended,

00:12:16.060 --> 00:12:17.830
and then it takes
offense if no one else

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:21.430
has the time or inclination.

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:25.510
It watches power structures,
it petitions for redress,

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.580
it gathers, rallies,
assembles queries,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:31.060
it blockades, occasionally
it even sincerely,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:34.397
with a good heart,
assaults in a good cause.

00:12:34.397 --> 00:12:36.230
It is an extraordinarily
hard working group.

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:38.210
It's never at rest.

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:41.150
Recently, it had
taken up the cause

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:44.762
of the inequality of income
distribution in North America,

00:12:44.762 --> 00:12:46.970
the preservation of all
stained glass window at Yale,

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.080
and women's volleyball.

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:52.489
Well, I was summoned to meet
this standing committee.

00:12:52.489 --> 00:12:54.530
I said I'd meet them in
a trustee room near where

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:55.602
I had my office.

00:12:55.602 --> 00:12:58.060
They said they weren't sure
they could all fit in the room.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.165
I said they could
send delegates.

00:13:00.165 --> 00:13:02.540
They said they didn't trust
each other enough to delegate

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:03.725
any of their number.

00:13:03.725 --> 00:13:05.120
And I said, well,
it's up to you.

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:06.530
They canceled.

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:08.240
Some clergymen in
the city immediately

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:09.470
petitioned on their behalf.

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:10.070
I caved in.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:14.210
And when they finally arrived,
there were only seven of them.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:15.380
I said, what could I do?

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:17.362
There's a long silence.

00:13:17.362 --> 00:13:18.070
What's the issue?

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:19.910
I was baffled.

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:21.879
And finally the
spokesperson said,

00:13:21.879 --> 00:13:23.920
we're really very sorry
to come to you like this,

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:28.114
but we are deeply concerned that
no one in the administration

00:13:28.114 --> 00:13:30.280
is paying any attention to
the most pressing problem

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:33.430
of our time, which is
the problem of evil

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:37.040
and the restoration of paradise.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:38.730
But I said, we
tried to solve that.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:41.930
I sent out a memo years ago.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:45.360
Well, we weren't here years
ago, said the spokesperson.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.500
We're here now.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:48.890
What can we do to
make it better?

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:52.810
And in fact, we
talked into the night.

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:56.830
I suppose in some ways, this
conversation and my versions

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.910
of the other conversations,
are variations

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:02.640
on that serious and
splendid conversation

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:06.190
that is any great university
anywhere in the country.

00:14:06.190 --> 00:14:08.590
University today is very
different from the one

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:11.800
25 years ago, or 50, or
100 or 250 years ago,

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.810
and yet it is not different.

00:14:14.810 --> 00:14:17.110
Tough as they are, they are
still fragile institutions.

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:20.620
But at heart, they are still
a constant conversation

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:24.250
between young and old, between
students and among faculty,

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:28.220
between faculty and students,
a conversation between past

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:32.750
and present, a conversation
the culture has with itself

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:34.890
on behalf of the country.

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:36.780
University lives
through all its voices,

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:39.720
and the conversation
does not stop there,

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:42.420
nor does each of our
conversations with whatever we

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:45.830
took away stop either.

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.320
It is perhaps, the sound
of all those voices

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:52.260
over centuries overlapping,
giving and taking

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:55.340
that is finally the
music of civilization,

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:59.450
the sound of human beings
shaping and sharing mooring

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:05.540
ideals to reality and making
the world for all its pain work.

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:09.240
The university is the place
where the mind learns first

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:14.570
how to make ideas which is the
mind's most durable product.

00:15:14.570 --> 00:15:17.540
University is neither a
paradise, nor the worst spot

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:19.580
we've ever been in.

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:21.470
It is a good place,
which continues to want

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:24.040
to make our children better.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:25.960
But its essence is
that give and take,

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:31.290
that civil conversation
in its innumerable forms.

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:34.140
When that conversation,
the to and fro of ideas

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:37.920
is stymied or
foreclosed or frozen,

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.480
when the questing
for truth is told

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.260
that it must cease because
there is only one truth

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:46.370
and it is complete, then the
institution in its essence,

00:15:46.370 --> 00:15:50.450
is chilled, and its
life threatened.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:52.400
The enemy of the
university is finally

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:58.170
not dissent, not disagreement,
not disagreeableness Gentility,

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:00.450
after all is the mark of a
great finishing school, not

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:01.890
a great university.

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:05.230
University doesn't
care for the genteel.

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:09.410
It cares for the blood
and sinews of ideas,

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:13.170
and non-coercive combat
with other ideas.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:15.720
I suppose the non-coercive
quality is fundamentally

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:16.625
the key.

00:16:16.625 --> 00:16:20.460
It's a combat that doesn't
seek to take a life,

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:23.190
but seeks to add
energy, passion, logic,

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:26.670
and commitment to the open
life of the mind in the service

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:27.810
of a more just society.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:32.830
That's basically the nature and
purpose, the norm, the guiding

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:36.210
principle for all of us.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:40.290
The deniers of left or
right, the diagnostician

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:41.940
for whom all
illnesses is similar,

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:45.500
because all cures are
identical, the purveyors

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:49.250
of an ism, the dealers and
system, the myopic for whom all

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:50.990
the world's pain
is simply reduced

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:54.260
to their cause, the simplifiers
who tell you that they're

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:57.170
idealistic because they boil
life down to a bumper sticker

00:16:57.170 --> 00:17:00.610
or a T-shirt maxim,
the reductionist

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:03.710
who pretend to global
concerns so as to promote

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:05.390
a personal preoccupation.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:07.609
These are in some
ways the enemies

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:11.710
of give and take, of debate,
disagreement of dissent,

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:15.940
the shouters who want it now
care of nothing for exchange,

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:21.930
for connection, for each of us
each to each working it out.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:24.210
What must be fluid
so that each of us

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:27.810
has the freedom to promote
another's freedom of mind

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.090
and spirit and
belief, they would

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:35.280
freeze, catching us all in
the amber of their dogma.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.040
Some ways they are the subtlest
enemies of the university,

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.770
of the life of the mind, not
because they win the day,

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:45.530
but because they remove
themselves from debate.

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:50.870
They force us into us and them,
fragmenting precisely when they

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:54.710
most hunger for solidarity,
splintering the very sense

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:57.759
of community they ache to form.

00:17:57.759 --> 00:17:59.300
You've encountered
this impulse here.

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:01.220
You would have in any
university or college,

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:04.140
and you will certainly
in the wider world.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:06.620
So all I learned in the
university, my graduating

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.860
friends, it is worth passing
on to you is simply this.

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:12.320
Do not write off the dogmatists.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:18.030
Do not acquiesce in
the apocalyptic style.

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:23.220
Insist on conversation, even
when it is not proffered.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:26.940
Have the courage to
connect, the courage

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:31.770
to strive to keep the shouting
down and the conversation open,

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:36.060
because I think only
in that way eventually

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:41.160
will equality of sexes and races
and opportunity finally come.

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:43.470
Only that way will
the homeless get

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:47.040
housed and the hungry fed
and the poor will get work

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:49.890
and will the city be rebuilt.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:55.030
To have the moral courage,
to avoid the selfishness

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:58.800
of self-righteousness,
and to assert positively

00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:01.410
the need we each of
us has for the other,

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:05.240
that is the real
work of humankind.

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:09.070
It has begun here,
and it will, I know,

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:13.120
be carried out into a life that
will remember how inhumane it

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:18.310
is to leave another alone.

00:19:18.310 --> 00:19:20.110
Good luck.

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:22.860
[APPLAUSE]

